    passageText: `Faithful transmission of genetic information requires effective DNA repair systems that correct damage caused by replication errors, reactive oxygen species, UV radiation, and chemical mutagens. Several distinct repair pathways have evolved to handle different types of lesions.

Base excision repair (BER) corrects small, non-helix-distorting lesions such as oxidized or deaminated bases. A DNA glycosylase recognizes and removes the damaged base, leaving an apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) site. AP endonuclease then cleaves the phosphodiester backbone, followed by DNA polymerase β filling in the correct nucleotide, and DNA ligase sealing the nick.

Nucleotide excision repair (NER) corrects bulky, helix-distorting lesions, most notably UV-induced cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers (CPDs) and 6-4 photoproducts. NER involves a recognition complex that identifies helix distortion, followed by dual incision removing an oligonucleotide of about 25-30 bases, gap-filling by DNA polymerase δ/ε, and ligation. Defects in NER cause xeroderma pigmentosum (XP), a condition characterized by extreme UV sensitivity and very high skin cancer risk.

Mismatch repair (MMR) corrects replication errors — mismatched base pairs and small insertion/deletion loops — that escape proofreading by DNA polymerase. MMR proteins recognize the mismatch, excise a patch of DNA from the newly synthesized strand, and resynthesize the correct sequence. Defects in MMR are associated with hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (Lynch syndrome).`,
